00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:51.080
but just being there as that voice

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.010
and that empathetic support

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:54.870
to really say, "Okay,
these are challenging times

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.330
and this is not something
that we can just ignore

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:58.383
and plow through".

00:10:58.383 --> 00:11:00.010
I mean, I think in a lot of ways

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:01.850
that ties into this conversation,

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:03.790
the "Intersection of
Mental Health and Faith",

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:05.720
I mean, I'll pass it on to
Pastor Mike and Dr. Green

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:09.500
to elaborate on but I would
say even in my own experiences

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:11.700
in academic settings in the community,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:13.890
oftentimes there isn't
that acknowledgement

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:16.947
at the first pass, so I've
talked to students who've said,

00:11:16.947 --> 00:11:18.840
"oh, my aunt is struggling with depression

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:19.673
and she's being told

00:11:19.673 --> 00:11:21.340
the only reason she's not getting better,

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:23.470
is because she isn't praying hard enough".

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:25.290
So yes, we're acknowledging
the power of God

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:28.090
but not acknowledging all the
tools we have at our disposal

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:29.750
to address those things

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.460
but then at the same time
I'll be teaching students

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:33.780
who will get very excited
about the biology of the brain

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.920
and how that impacts mental health

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.650
but say things like, "What
are we supposed to do

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.660
with those people that think
a higher power can help them

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:41.790
with their mental health challenges

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.200
or being a part of a faith community?"

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.160
So again, now being totally dismissive

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.930
about the other side of things

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:49.940
and I think oftentimes there's
not enough conversation

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:52.690
about how we need to look at
those things holistically.

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:54.440
And so that's one of the
reasons I'm so excited

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:55.840
about this conversation and the work

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.000
that you all have been involved
in, either on the ground

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.770
or as admirers and advocates of the work

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:03.743
and so, Pastor Mike and Dr. Green,

00:12:03.743 --> 00:12:06.120
I just wanted to hear a
little bit about the journey

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:09.280
into that space of even
creating that HOPE Center.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.130
I mean, this is the first
faith-based mental health center

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:13.727
in Harlem, it's unique in a lot of ways

00:12:13.727 --> 00:12:16.677
and I'm curious how all that
came about in the first place.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.250
- Well, I'll talk about that piece

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:23.670
and when we get to the exciting
work of the HOPE Center,

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:25.893
I'll let Dr. Green jump on it.

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:30.410
The journey really started,
towards the HOPE Center,

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:31.653
as personal.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.940
I suffer with a rare disease

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:40.940
it's called CVID, Common
Variable Immunodeficiency

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:44.403
and basically, I was
born with no antibodies,

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:49.130
with a very low life expectancy

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:51.100
but what it has meant over the years,

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.660
is numerous infections,
numerous hospitalizations,

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.120
surgeries, you name it, near
death experiences, sepsis,

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:01.173
I've survived sepsis three times,

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:05.520
two of the three times,
(mumbles) was MRSA,

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.140
which is the worst bacteria to get.

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:11.030
I mean, so over the years what it caused

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:12.373
is a lot of depression.

00:13:13.541 --> 00:13:16.610
When you're battling with chronic
illness, chronic sickness,

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.390
chronic pain, it has an impact

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:22.920
and I was dealing with all this
while pastoring in many ways

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:23.753
and it got to be a lot

00:13:23.753 --> 00:13:28.570
and I found myself in and
out of depressive moments.

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:30.420
And I never at that time,

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:34.310
this is going back into
the early 2000s and beyond,

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:36.260
never sought out a therapist,

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:38.300
I think not that I was against therapy,

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:40.900
I just never thought to
look for a therapist.

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.310
I figured, I can handle it, I'm a pastor,

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:44.640
I preach every week, I teach,

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:47.820
I lead people through these
moments, I can come out of it

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:48.653
and then

00:13:48.653 --> 00:13:52.610
in 2010

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:57.090
or seven rather, I was on a
trip to Seattle Washington

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:01.670
and I was staying in a hotel
on the 22nd floor of the hotel

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:04.600
and nothing, there was
nothing going on that day

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:06.430
that seemed strange or different

00:14:07.828 --> 00:14:10.540
and I remember going out on the balcony

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:14.913
and I literally heard a
voice tell me, say to me,

00:14:16.207 --> 00:14:18.057
"If you jump, you can survive".

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:20.170
That's what I heard.

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:22.510
It was the first time
I heard anything close

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:24.350
to suicidal ideation ever.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:27.410
The voice just said, "If
you jump, you can survive"

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:31.050
and I remember kind of
feeling another presence

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.790
kinda pull me back into
this room off the balcony

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.670
and I remember laying in this bed

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:37.620
almost in a fetal position, just crying.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:39.400
I didn't know what just happened

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:42.450
but it was very overwhelming
because in my entire life,

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:43.850
I never thought of that idea.

00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:45.600
I had never conceived of
it, never entered my mind

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.600
about the notion of taking my own life

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.447
and I was able to understand
that's what was just happening

00:14:51.447 --> 00:14:53.060
and it was literally a voice

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:55.110
as clear as you and I
are talking now Dr. Nii,

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:58.170
I heard it that, "If you
jump, you can survive".

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.390
And so that was kind of the first alarm

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:05.010
that something is not well

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:07.040
and again, still pushing through it,

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:10.780
still fighting through it,
still not seeing a therapist,

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:12.520
while at the same time
people are coming to me

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:14.970
for pastoral care and counseling,

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:18.590
while at the same time, in 2012,

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:22.720
Dr. Kinghorn, I was in Durham to preach

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.180
and got sick on a Wednesday,

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:27.050
at Duke Medical Center by Friday

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:30.300
and spent 14 days at Duke
Medical Center with sepsis

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:33.120
and I had MRSA and they had
no idea whether I'd survive.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:33.953
- Wow.

00:15:33.953 --> 00:15:38.010
- And when I came out of that in 2012,

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:41.170
I remember when I got better,
someone wanted to see me

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:43.790
about some issues they were
dealing with personally

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:48.110
and over the years, we as the
pastors had always gotten,

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:49.310
had sessions with people

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:51.320
whose issues were beyond
our ability to handle,

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.490
we found ourselves always referring

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:56.640
to spaces where they can
get mad mental health care

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:01.640
and I said to myself, one,
I need to find a therapist

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.840
and then I said, there are people

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:07.410
who are coming to the church
who are in need of care

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:09.340
beyond what we're capable of giving.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.990
So when I started my journey in therapy,

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:16.920
is when we looked to hire a
part-time therapist on staff

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:19.950
to deal with some of the issues

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:22.570
that were beyond our scope as pastors.

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:25.170
All of us were trained,
all of us had pastoral care

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.570
and counseling, clinical
pastoral education

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:30.030
but there was still things
we just could not deal with,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:33.040
so we hired her, her name was
at the time, Joyce Johnson

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:35.570
and it was pretty soon she got swamped.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:39.310
She was there and then she
got overloaded with things

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:42.490
but one day when I was at
the church in the lobby

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:44.800
because when you come
to the information desk,

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:46.330
in the entrance,

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.020
the receptionist is like,
"Who are you here to see?"

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:50.950
Joyce had an office upstairs in the church

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:52.020
and I'm sitting in the lobby,

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.380
the person didn't know who I was,

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:58.200
they were not a member of the
church and when they came in

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:00.670
and when the receptionist asked the person

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:03.973
who were they coming to see,
they whispered Joyce's name.

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:08.300
I perceived in that moment,
there was still some shame,

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:12.240
like I'm coming here to see
someone about some issues

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:15.470
and the stigma, well, I'm
not crazy and nothing's wrong

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:16.520
but I noticed them whispering.

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:20.230
And I realized at that
moment, and this was in 2000,

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:23.660
towards maybe the beginning of 2013,

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:27.890
I said, having this space is not enough,

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:30.290
I wanna have a freestanding space

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:31.970
so people felt comfortable going in

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:33.870
because if you come to church
and you go to see Joyce,

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:35.631
people will think you're crazy.

00:17:35.631 --> 00:17:37.550
That's what I was feeling

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:40.250
and so I had been looking,

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:42.490
so we have the Dream Center
that you talked about,

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:45.100
which is really cutting edge,
we do a lot of programming

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.530
but we got the space
because of a relationship

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:50.800
with one of our trustees
who is also a developer

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:53.150
and when he asked about
getting some community support

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:55.060
for this project he had,

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:56.590
I said, "Well, look, we're
going to give you support

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.750
but I got an idea, that
first floor of your building,

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:00.830
let us get that building.

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:02.790
We'll pay rent and we used
it for the Dream Center".

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:07.790
Well, in 2014, he needed
support for another space

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:09.500
around the corner from the church,

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.590
I said, "Listen, let's do the same thing.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:14.757
Let's get this space", in that
moment it was like instant,

00:18:14.757 --> 00:18:18.880
"let's use this space for
a mental health facility"

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:21.540
and that's where it started.

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:24.620
And for about a year and a
half, they worked on the space,

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:26.210
we were able to build it out

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:30.513
and then we had the ribbon
cutting in December of 2014,

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:36.760
16 rather for the HOPE
Center and we named it HOPE,

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.920
we had Dream and now we had HOPE,

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:42.320
Healing On Purpose and Evolving

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:44.340
where we offered
initially, we've changed it

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:48.100
but initially people who
came into the HOPE Center

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:50.470
and went through the process,
we didn't call them clients,

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:51.870
we called them HOPE Innovators.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:53.970
We wanted to change the language,

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:58.740
persons who are creatively
finding paths towards healing

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.730
and wholeness, just changing the language,

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:04.110
changing the culture, changing the dynamic

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:05.630
but initially when you came

00:19:05.630 --> 00:19:07.420
and you went through the
program and the application,

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:11.260
you got 12 free therapeutic
sessions, plus followup,

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:14.750
plus potentially involved
in group sessions as well.

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:16.120
So it was something that
I had never been done,

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:19.240
I mean, when you think about
the cost of 12 sessions

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:22.220
of therapy and we were offering
this for free initially,

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:25.024
we had some people who, we had a staff

00:19:25.024 --> 00:19:28.290
but then we had two
volunteers, two women who...

00:19:29.557 --> 00:19:32.040
it was amazing, they
had visited the church

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.470
and they weren't Christian,
they were actually Jewish

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:36.960
but they had been visiting
the church quite a bit,

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:41.040
they really liked the church
and they were psychiatrists

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:43.180
and so they offered their services.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:44.417
- Wow.

00:19:44.417 --> 00:19:46.950
- But like that first
year or so, year or two,

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:48.830
one got ill with cancer

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:50.400
but they were offering their services free

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:52.610
and because of their
presence, they would offer

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:54.870
to prescribe medication if necessary.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.620
So we had our staff, our clinician,

00:19:57.620 --> 00:19:59.660
plus we had these two psychiatrists

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:02.210
volunteering their services every weekend,

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:04.250
so it was amazing how things unfolded

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:06.946
and persons started making
donations and we were...

00:20:06.946 --> 00:20:09.063
I mean, it was just great.

00:20:09.063 --> 00:20:11.010
And 'cause I was of the belief
that if we build this space,

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:14.050
people will come and it was
to meet a particular need

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:16.590
because you know the stigma
in the Black community

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:20.860
that exists around mental
health and mental health issues

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:24.380
and so we wanted to engage
in de-stigmatizing that

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:27.780
and then I from the
pulpit, began preaching

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:32.560
about my personal battles
with depression and anxiety

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:33.393
and that opened up

00:20:33.393 --> 00:20:36.610
what I thought if the
pastor can be transparent,

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:39.380
if I can talk about it,
it'll free other people up

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:41.740
to feel, hey, it's okay to talk about it

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:43.400
and it's okay to acknowledge it

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:46.389
and by the way, then we have a space

00:20:46.389 --> 00:20:48.160
that can help you navigate this.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:50.690
So yeah, I'll stop there for now.

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.640
- Wow, that's so powerful,
that's amazing to hear that story

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:54.690
just from your personal journey

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.010
and everything that came out
in the community as well.

00:20:57.010 --> 00:21:00.210
I think it's so important that
you are acknowledging that

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:01.582
from the pulpit as well

00:21:01.582 --> 00:21:03.360
'cause I think that helps
people in those moments

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:05.420
to know it's not a quick fix.

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:07.880
So I'm sure that's some of
what comes into it as well.

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:09.580
I mean, when I shared about that student,

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:13.760
basically, it's the mindset
that it hasn't been fixed yet

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:16.300
because you're not putting in
enough effort in your prayer

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:17.170
for God to address that

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:19.173
but you mentioned that from the pulpit

00:21:19.173 --> 00:21:20.420
and so people know the journey

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:22.780
and I think that's so important.

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:25.370
- Yeah, and I had those
same comments before this

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:29.480
and I had a woman say to me one time,

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:32.450
a member of the church,
she said, "Well, pastor,

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:35.897
I understand what you're
doing but I got to trust God

00:21:35.897 --> 00:21:38.780
and I believe demons",

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:40.500
and she went through this whole thing.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.280
And I asked her,

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:46.700
I said, "Do you have
any physical ailments?"

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:50.760
And she told me, "Yes", she
said, "I have diabetes".

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:52.440
I said, "So is God dealing with that

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:53.590
or you got medication?"

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:57.770
And she said, "Well,
I take insulin shots".

00:21:57.770 --> 00:22:01.670
I said, "Oh", I said, "Okay,
so you trust the doctors

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:04.590
with the diabetes but you
don't trust practitioners

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:07.610
with mental health issues?"
and she just paused

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:10.513
and she just paused and
that was the reality.

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:14.280
There's no stigma

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.530
around going to the
hospital when you're sick

00:22:18.058 --> 00:22:19.490
and we often talk about that.

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:21.440
You break your arm,
nobody's saying, "Oh my God,

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:22.930
you going to go to the
hospital to get that fixed?"

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:25.980
No, but there's a stigma around
issues around the spirit,

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:29.660
around the mind, around it soul
and we have to do that work

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:32.870
of really, like I said, de-mythologizing

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:35.770
and de-stigmatizing those issues

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:37.567
and I think the more we do it

00:22:37.567 --> 00:22:39.580
and the more we've been talking about it,

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:42.810
we've seen a change in
the language of people

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:43.643
in the community.

00:22:43.643 --> 00:22:46.910
And also having a space, it's
one thing to talk about it

00:22:46.910 --> 00:22:50.280
from the pulpit and to make
it a priority to the church,

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:52.500
it's another thing to have a space

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:56.340
that you can say, "By the
way, here's the therefore,

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:59.150
here's the therefore,
therefore there's this space".

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:00.563
- Yeah, that's so important,

00:23:02.256 --> 00:23:05.990
I'm so honored to be, just
hearing about the work

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:07.080
that you've been doing on the ground

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.830
and the sense to really set this up.

00:23:08.830 --> 00:23:10.870
I was gonna pivot to Dr.
Green and hear a little bit

00:23:10.870 --> 00:23:13.190
about some of that as well and
even just, one of the points

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:14.980
that Pastor Mike, that you brought up

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:15.910
as I was listening to it,

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:17.760
it seemed like that tension was ongoing.

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:19.550
You talked about the
first person who came in

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:21.520
who was volunteering, who was overwhelmed,

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:23.180
so obviously there's a need..

00:23:23.180 --> 00:23:24.840
- Well, she wasn't volunteering,
she was being paid...

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:27.260
- She was getting paid...
- We were paying her,

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:28.310
she worked part-time.

00:23:29.203 --> 00:23:31.153
We wanted to make sure
we got good services.

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:35.580
- So even as she's doing
that, being overwhelmed

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.510
but then people are still
whispering about going to see her.

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:40.270
So it seems like both of
those were kinda happening

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:42.090
at the same time.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:46.050
So Dr. Green, like how
has that moved forward

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:47.550
on the ground level as a director?

00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:48.850
How do you see that play out?

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:51.190
Do you feel like you're dealing
with both simultaneously

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:53.260
or people are getting better
at acknowledging the need

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:55.650
or are you still trying to
break through the stigma?

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:58.480
What's that whole process
like at this point?

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:01.797
- Yes, I would say it's
a little bit of both.

00:24:01.797 --> 00:24:03.363
And so for me,

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:07.480
my mantra has always been you can pray

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:08.563
and go to therapy.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.410
So I'm also very active in the community

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:15.370
and always have been and
so people have always known

00:24:15.370 --> 00:24:18.670
that I'm a mental health
clinician, I'm a psychotherapist

00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:20.520
and so having those
conversations with people

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:23.470
has always been something
that's been super easy for me.

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:27.420
And so pastor brought up the
example of talking to the woman

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:32.330
about her medical condition and
would she go to the hospital

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:33.620
to seek treatment for that?

00:24:33.620 --> 00:24:35.190
And that's a great place to start

00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:37.560
because I think it's
helping people to unpack

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:41.990
their thought process around
mental health treatment

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:44.800
and there are all these things
that sort of go in there.

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:48.250
But part of it also too, is
this, I think this conspiracy

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:51.870
of silence around the
mental health challenges

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:55.170
that people have experienced
for many, many years

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:58.210
and haven't really had an
opportunity to talk about it,

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:01.060
we can talk about the
issues around discrimination

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:03.440
and racism that have also
plagued the mental health system

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:06.010
that has sort of impacted people's ability

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:09.033
to trust the systems of
care that are in place,

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:13.180
feeling like they can also
seek treatment and places

00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:15.393
and with people who also look like them.

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:19.100
So all of those things
sort of go into that.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:20.770
And so I think we do a good job

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:24.630
at keeping the conversation
open and transparent and clear

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:26.920
and also not taking
ourselves too seriously.

00:25:26.920 --> 00:25:30.220
Like just saying, "Hey,
we have this thing here,

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:31.800
it's free and it's available"

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:34.360
and I'll say things to folks
like, "Where can you go

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:35.980
and get some things for free?

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:37.950
How often can you go and do that?"

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:39.180
And they're like, "Well, no..."

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:41.393
and then I may say to them,
"What are some of the things

00:25:41.393 --> 00:25:42.780
that are important to you?"

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:44.880
When I'm talking to
some of the millennials

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:46.030
who wanna have a conversation

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:47.994
around the cost of mental health

00:25:47.994 --> 00:25:49.994
and I say, "Well, how often do you shop?

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:54.720
How much did you pay for
the last pair of shoes?

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:57.830
And the same ways in
which you go for brunch

00:25:57.830 --> 00:26:02.160
or you go shopping, it's
building space into your life

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:03.180
and making spaces in your life

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:04.430
for things that are important to you,

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:05.760
so how do you prioritize that?

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.060
And then if your mental
health is not a priority,

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:09.620
well, now let's talk about that too".

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:11.950
So there are always these
ongoing conversations

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:16.710
around how to open up the door
just to have the conversation

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:18.230
and to make it welcoming.

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:20.840
And so we've done our
best to do that here,

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:23.220
so we offer the sessions

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:26.580
but one of the things that
have been important to me,

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:29.985
is making sure that we have a
ton of public-facing programs

00:26:29.985 --> 00:26:30.818
as well.

00:26:30.818 --> 00:26:34.090
And so we have right now implemented

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:37.490
what we call "our healing
conversations" on a monthly basis

00:26:37.490 --> 00:26:42.290
and we tackle really tough
issues at least once a month

00:26:42.290 --> 00:26:45.130
and so we've tackled suicide,

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:47.090
we've tackled domestic violence,

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:50.370
we've tackled grief and
loss, actually last month,

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:52.590
this month we're gonna
be talking about anxiety

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:54.420
and then in June,

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:55.900
which will be our last
healing conversation,

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:58.200
we're gonna be talking about
the importance of sleep

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:01.983
and rest and what does that
mean in our daily lives?

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:06.260
I've also been able to develop
some fantastic partnerships

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:09.070
with a bunch of organizations
in the community,

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:12.020
we don't see children here,
the folks that we see here

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:14.810
are 18 and over, so it's
been important for us

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:18.360
to cultivate relationships with
places that provide services

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:21.050
to adolescents and children and
so we've been in the process

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:22.650
of tying to develop that as well

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:26.610
but one point with regard to the need,

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:28.750
is that when I came
here to the HOPE Center,

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:31.140
we had a waiting list of 76 people.

00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:32.040
- Wow!

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:34.750
- And some of those folks
had been on the waiting list

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:36.550
for over a year.

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:38.390
And so one of my first priorities

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:41.870
was to sort of look at that
list, go back to those folks

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:43.830
and prioritize like who's sort of

00:27:43.830 --> 00:27:47.040
in high need of care
services, who was in crisis.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:49.250
And then we were also still
in the middle of COVID,

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:51.550
so how could we then provide
programming that folks,

00:27:51.550 --> 00:27:54.260
people who were also suffering from loss

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:56.500
and so we've been in the
process of doing that.

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:59.380
So two relationships that we've built

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:04.380
that I've been so proud to
participate in is our partnership

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:08.460
with Columbia University
Center for Complicated Grief,

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:10.950
so all of my staff here,
including the interns

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:13.573
have trained in
complicated grief services,

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:16.900
they also get rigorous suicide training

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:18.970
and mental health counseling
training of course,

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:20.073
I'm a field instructor as well,

00:28:20.073 --> 00:28:22.037
as well as a professor at NYU

00:28:22.037 --> 00:28:25.960
and I'm an adjunct faculty
there, so I get to make sure

00:28:25.960 --> 00:28:29.480
that I'm being consistent
with the kinds of training

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:32.300
and teaching that students need here

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:35.040
to be successful clinicians
out in the community.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:39.600
We also make sure that we pay
attention to issues of race,

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:42.700
issues of power and
privilege, all of those things

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:46.260
and making sure that the staff
here is also quite diverse.

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.700
And so we do all of that, we make sure

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:52.430
that we are paying attention
to our own mental health needs,

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.610
so when Pastor Mike was
talking, one of the things

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:59.240
that has always been taught
to me by my own mentors

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:01.110
is that the healers need healing

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:03.740
and the healers need spaces to go as well.

00:29:03.740 --> 00:29:07.650
And so making supervision
part of, and therapy,

00:29:07.650 --> 00:29:09.580
a consistent part of your life.

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:14.580
And so that's why this year,
we came up with our tagline

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:17.920
for our theme this year, which
is "Healing is a Lifestyle"

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:19.810
to really cultivate and encourage folks

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:21.803
to continue on that path for wellness

00:29:21.803 --> 00:29:23.353
and that journey to wellness.

00:29:24.764 --> 00:29:25.597
- That's so great,

00:29:25.597 --> 00:29:26.780
I mean, that's such a
holistic approach too

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:28.940
and you're tackling so
many things at once,

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:29.810
which are so critical.

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:32.950
I mean, even dealing with
the stigma on the front end,

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:34.910
making sure that you're covering
all the different topics

00:29:34.910 --> 00:29:37.320
that influence mental
health including sleep,

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:40.060
making sure that you're
talking about the impact

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:42.450
of the racial injustices and racism

00:29:42.450 --> 00:29:45.000
and then partnering with those outside,

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:46.370
so I think in so many ways,

00:29:46.370 --> 00:29:49.217
this model that you all have
developed is so important

00:29:49.217 --> 00:29:50.710
and so effective.

00:29:50.710 --> 00:29:52.810
And so Dr. Kinghorn, I'm actually curious,

00:29:52.810 --> 00:29:55.740
as someone who's been an
admirer and an advocate

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:57.570
of this work, what are some of the things

00:29:57.570 --> 00:29:58.550
that have stood out to you

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:01.940
about just the importance of
the work that they're doing

00:30:01.940 --> 00:30:04.450
in Harlem and what has caught your eye

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:07.630
as someone who has your
foot in both worlds,

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:11.233
both in faith community
and as a psychiatrist?

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:13.987
- Thank you, yeah, I've
been a distant admirer

00:30:13.987 --> 00:30:16.250
of the HOPE Center for quite a while,

00:30:16.250 --> 00:30:17.710
I first learned about HOPE Center

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:20.020
through the work of my
colleague and psychiatrist,

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:22.880
Sidney Hankerson at Columbia University,

00:30:22.880 --> 00:30:25.800
who is doing really unique research,

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:28.660
a federally-funded research on
the role of faith communities

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:31.960
and especially of churches
in depression intervention

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:35.680
and I've learned a lot
from Dr. Hankerson's work.

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:38.730
One of his early published
papers, which he published

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:42.530
in the Journal of Urban
Health was a focus group study

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:45.880
with pastors at a church
in New York and Queens,

00:30:45.880 --> 00:30:47.310
so it wasn't First Corinthians Baptist

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:50.450
but I interviewed a lot of of pastors

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:51.460
around their experience

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.950
in a predominantly
African-American large church

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:57.110
in New York City around how
they encountered depression

00:30:57.110 --> 00:31:00.850
and these pastors recognized
that depression was present

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:01.683
in their community,

00:31:01.683 --> 00:31:04.070
they wanted the church
to be a site of support

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:06.637
and they also really
helpfully though said,

00:31:06.637 --> 00:31:08.200
"We have to think about depression

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:11.160
not just as like an
individual medical problem

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:14.200
that needs treatment but
we need to also situate it

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:17.060
in the context of the lived
realities of people's lives;

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:21.053
of jobs stress, of
financial stress, of racism,

00:31:22.166 --> 00:31:26.410
of problems with education
and accessing medical care",

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:29.710
and it pointed to the
way in which churches

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:34.580
can actually be sites of outreach
and support and connection

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:38.890
even more but partnering
with medical systems

00:31:38.890 --> 00:31:42.300
but how can churches be, do
that kind of connecting work

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:45.110
and especially not just recognizing

00:31:45.110 --> 00:31:46.990
the way in which mental
health problems don't exist

00:31:46.990 --> 00:31:50.420
in a vacuum, they take
place in a kind of community

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:52.433
and culture and sets of relationships.

00:31:53.298 --> 00:31:54.920
And so when I hear about the HOPE Center

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:58.610
being situated within a church,
made possible by a church,

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:01.320
providing pathways to high
quality mental health care

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:03.960
and also they're responding to the needs

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:05.620
of the local community,

00:32:05.620 --> 00:32:09.310
which in some ways is the
needs of our entire country,

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:10.640
I'm just really excited about it

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:14.060
and just really have been
so excited to see it grow

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:16.300
over the years and to hear about its work

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:19.290
and to hear more about
it through my connections

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:21.323
at Duke Divinity School with Pastor Mike.

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:25.330
- Yeah, that's so well said
and it's so encouraging to hear

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:28.150
about the work that's being
done on multiple fronts

00:32:28.150 --> 00:32:29.550
and some of these connections as well.

00:32:29.550 --> 00:32:32.410
I know Dr. Hankerson has
been very well connected

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:33.243
to this initiative

00:32:33.243 --> 00:32:36.297
and I think there's so much
that others can learn as well.

00:32:36.297 --> 00:32:37.800
And so I'm actually curious to know,

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:39.210
as you all have been moving through this

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:41.200
and kinda breaking down
some of these barriers

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:42.640
and empowering people,

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:47.520
are there aspects that you
think can be expanded and used

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.438
and applied in other places as well

00:32:49.438 --> 00:32:51.270
or is this something that's
unique to the environment

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:53.210
that you have in Harlem
and the relationships there

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:55.190
or are there principles that you see

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:57.390
that could actually be
replicated by others

00:32:57.390 --> 00:32:59.070
in other parts of the country?

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:01.420
- Well, a few things,

00:33:01.420 --> 00:33:02.253
one,

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:08.420
well, two things actually,
one, I've had conversations

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:12.520
with other colleagues in
ministry, about the HOPE Center

00:33:13.580 --> 00:33:18.380
and wanting to do this,
wanting to try to replicate it

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:20.630
in some way, shape or form

00:33:20.630 --> 00:33:25.630
and the conversation at some
point turns into a conversation

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:29.550
about church budgets
and I'll tell you why,

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:32.230
because the first question is resources.

00:33:32.230 --> 00:33:35.880
We used to be told, "Budgets
are moral documents"

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:40.880
and I would say, "No, budgets
are vision documents to me",

00:33:41.801 --> 00:33:44.560
where do you see yourself,
where do you wanna go

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:46.900
as a church, and the reason
why I brought this up

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:49.510
is because when we opened the HOPE Center

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:52.600
for the first few years,
there was no public money,

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:57.020
it was all church donations
that funded it, that staffed it,

00:33:57.020 --> 00:33:58.620
that kept it going.

00:33:58.620 --> 00:34:02.750
And what we made a priority
for the church initially,

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:05.610
is that this was important,
this was meeting a need

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:10.610
in the community and we put
our resources where the need is

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:12.733
and this was a great need.

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:17.230
And so the people believed in
it, the church believed in it,

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:18.810
the leadership believed in it

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:20.150
and were able to give to it

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:21.970
to make sure that it was able to function

00:34:21.970 --> 00:34:23.620
and sustain itself.

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:24.750
That's the first thing.

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:27.250
I think a lot of times in ministry,

00:34:27.250 --> 00:34:31.140
we do ministry based on
ideas, not always need

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:33.540
and so I had to tell a
lot of my colleagues that

00:34:34.490 --> 00:34:36.070
and it became important.

00:34:36.070 --> 00:34:40.670
Secondly, myself and Dr.
Hankerson and others have talked

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:44.270
about using the HOPE
Centers, I just kind of spoke

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.600
in real model, it can be replicated.

00:34:46.600 --> 00:34:50.010
I think if more faith-based institutions

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:54.140
had this kind of presence
in the community,

00:34:54.140 --> 00:34:58.720
it could go a long way
again, in de-stigmatizing

00:34:59.680 --> 00:35:02.420
the narrative and what you find

00:35:02.420 --> 00:35:05.300
is that many faith-based
institutions want to do it

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:07.980
but they begin talking about resources.

00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:10.870
So I think there are institutions

00:35:10.870 --> 00:35:12.960
that have the capacity to do it

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:16.950
but I think there are tons
of resources out there

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:18.860
for people to begin these initiatives

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:21.690
but I do think there's a
willingness by many clergy.

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:23.270
So that's one of my dreams,

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:26.230
to see HOPE Center is in other places.

00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:28.610
I've talked with Dr. Green about this,

00:35:28.610 --> 00:35:31.760
that how do we make this happen

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:34.120
in other parts of the city for now

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:37.860
and then make it something
that transcends the city

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:41.690
because it serves a great need,
listen, here's the reality,

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:42.860
we're doing a great work,

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:45.240
Dr. Green and her team
are doing a great work

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:47.580
but we we're still, in
some ways overwhelmed

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:50.200
and we've run out of space.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:53.170
So it was like, when we
opened it from within,

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:55.470
I would say within the first nine months

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:57.430
we realized we were already
tapped off of space.

00:35:57.430 --> 00:35:58.771
- Wow!

00:35:58.771 --> 00:36:03.070
- So we, Dr. Green has
talked to me about that,

00:36:03.070 --> 00:36:06.283
about needing a bigger
space, which is a good thing,

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:09.410
so that we can offer more services

00:36:09.410 --> 00:36:11.020
and have a more adequate space,

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:14.790
conducive for the kind of
clinical work that's being done,

00:36:14.790 --> 00:36:16.083
so I'm just,

00:36:16.983 --> 00:36:20.300
I'm grateful for these challenges we have

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:24.200
but I'm also grateful
for the possibilities

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:27.570
and I'm grateful for Dr. Green

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.500
because I think she not
only has the ability

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:34.130
but the vision to make the
larger vision come to pass

00:36:34.130 --> 00:36:35.130
for the HOPE Center.

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:41.760
- I just wanna add too about,
with regard to resources

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:43.810
and I wasn't there in the beginning,

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:46.730
I've been connected to First
Corinthians Baptist Church

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:49.780
since 2006 and working in
ministry under Pastor Mike

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:53.340
for quite a while before
I came to the HOPE Center.

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:54.940
But one of the things that I will say

00:36:54.940 --> 00:36:57.560
is that just beginning the conversations

00:36:57.560 --> 00:37:00.210
around mental health and
seeing what resources

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:03.000
are within your own congregation

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:04.550
because I think you would be surprised

00:37:04.550 --> 00:37:05.383
about

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:09.520
what may come up.

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:12.210
Pastor Mike talked about
these two psychiatrists

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:14.920
who decided that they
wanted to donate their time.

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:17.520
So I think just beginning there
and having the conversation

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:20.057
and being open about the
kinds of things that you need

00:37:20.057 --> 00:37:22.400
and the kinds of things
that you'd like to see,

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:25.710
I wanted to mention also
too, that with Dr. Hankerson,

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:27.740
one of the things that we did at FCBC

00:37:27.740 --> 00:37:31.210
was do this mental health
first aid training.

00:37:31.210 --> 00:37:35.520
And that started with Dr.
Hankerson training our pastors

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:39.590
and our deacons initially,
and then opening that up

00:37:39.590 --> 00:37:42.550
to other volunteers who were interested

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:44.110
in just having a basic understanding

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:46.710
to have a one-on-one on
mental health first aid

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:48.990
and sort of how to intervene in a crisis.

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:51.560
And so you don't necessarily
always have to have experts

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:55.640
on hand, that's a great
place for you to start

00:37:55.640 --> 00:37:57.810
and then that sort of
develops into other things

00:37:57.810 --> 00:38:01.270
and so one of the things
that we're doing now

00:38:01.270 --> 00:38:03.480
in partnership with Dr. Hankerson,

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:07.560
is in June, we're gonna
be embarking on a study

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:10.960
that focuses on understanding
the experiences of depression

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:11.793
in Black men.

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:14.530
And so we're really looking
forward to that because we know

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:17.140
that offer some additional
really good information

00:38:17.140 --> 00:38:20.460
about how to provide
resources and services

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:22.850
and engage men overall.

00:38:22.850 --> 00:38:25.540
So I think just again,
starting the conversation,

00:38:25.540 --> 00:38:27.050
seeing what resources you have within

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:31.356
and just starting, making the
commitment to start somewhere.

00:38:31.356 --> 00:38:32.189
- I think it's so great

00:38:32.189 --> 00:38:33.240
that you both really pulled that out...

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:35.030
- And I wanna add to that,

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:38.070
if you think about, I
remember when, this was before

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:42.320
the HOPE Center was really an
idea, we had Joyce Johnson,

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:44.510
Ms. Johnson and Dr. Hanks and I

00:38:44.510 --> 00:38:46.090
would have numerous conversations,

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:48.810
so when we did the mental
health first responders

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:51.060
kind of training, we had almost 300 people

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:52.110
that ended up being trained.

00:38:52.110 --> 00:38:53.420
- Wow!

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:56.220
- And it was amazing because we realized,

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:58.750
not only was there a need
but it was an interest

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:02.180
from the community to help
and to be a part of this.

00:39:02.180 --> 00:39:05.390
And it was amazing over
a period of some months

00:39:05.390 --> 00:39:06.590
these trainings took place

00:39:06.590 --> 00:39:08.380
but the amount of people who came out,

00:39:08.380 --> 00:39:10.326
I was surprised by the
numbers 'cause I didn't know

00:39:10.326 --> 00:39:11.870
it would be that many people

00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:13.540
but many people wanted to be part of this,

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:15.800
part of these kinds of healing efforts,

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:17.010
so I just had to know that

00:39:17.010 --> 00:39:19.375
because it was really impressive
when we first started that

00:39:19.375 --> 00:39:20.270
with Dr. Hankerson.

00:39:20.270 --> 00:39:21.103
- Yeah, that's great,

00:39:21.103 --> 00:39:22.530
that was actually gonna
be my next question

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:25.310
'cause I was curious just what
the reception had been like

00:39:25.310 --> 00:39:27.590
as we talked about just
people having a hesitancy,

00:39:27.590 --> 00:39:30.400
whether people were open
to that first aid training,

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:31.440
mental health first aid training,

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:32.410
did they feel like they needed it

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:34.490
but I mean, just those
numbers that you mentioned,

00:39:34.490 --> 00:39:37.550
it seems like both the need and
the desire were really there

00:39:37.550 --> 00:39:39.320
which I think is outstanding.

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:41.440
Dr. Kinghorn, I'll be
curious to hear your thoughts

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:42.460
on some of this as well,

00:39:42.460 --> 00:39:44.050
some of these topics
that we're talking about

00:39:44.050 --> 00:39:45.734
within the Harlem community,

00:39:45.734 --> 00:39:47.640
and what you've seen in
other parts of the country

00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:51.520
or even nationally in terms
of, one, is there an openness

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:54.650
to these conversations or
are people still resistant,

00:39:54.650 --> 00:39:57.500
either in the faith community
or in the medical community

00:39:58.370 --> 00:40:01.040
to really have these
integrated conversations?

00:40:01.040 --> 00:40:02.630
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:40:02.630 --> 00:40:05.690
I think the HOPE Center is
such a great model of success

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:07.790
even though I know
there's ongoing challenges

00:40:07.790 --> 00:40:09.923
around growth and sustainability,

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:14.500
in my, there are examples in
other parts of the country

00:40:14.500 --> 00:40:18.790
of mental health centers and
efforts that are church-led,

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.200
in my experience, they
tend to be successful

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:23.090
when three things are present.

00:40:23.090 --> 00:40:25.920
One, you got to have a
committed church leadership

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:29.900
that often leads from personal
experiences of church leaders

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:32.480
and it shouldn't be
housed in just one person,

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:34.470
like one pastor who's the champion

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:36.730
but it needs to be
something that's embraced

00:40:36.730 --> 00:40:40.720
by the community, we gotta
have a specific leader

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:43.570
of the initiative that has the
right kind of clinical wisdom

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:44.640
and training and skills

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:47.760
and I think both of those
things are well represented

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:50.930
on our call today by
Pastor Mike and Dr. Green

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:52.330
and you also have to have a center

00:40:52.330 --> 00:40:53.780
that understands its community

00:40:53.780 --> 00:40:56.070
and the needs of its
community and is responding

00:40:56.070 --> 00:40:58.470
to specifically what's
present in the community

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:00.850
and so these efforts
tend to look different

00:41:00.850 --> 00:41:03.020
in different places, in different cities,

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:04.620
depending on what the needs are.

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:07.740
With respect to the broader questions,

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:10.780
I think, around how
conversations about mental health

00:41:10.780 --> 00:41:11.940
are happening in churches

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:14.077
and I am interested in Pastor Mike's

00:41:14.077 --> 00:41:16.230
and Dr. Green's perspective on this,

00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:17.930
there's certainly a stereotype out there

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:19.470
that churches are not safe spaces

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:21.290
to talk about mental
health and mental illness

00:41:21.290 --> 00:41:23.630
and that stereotype has
a grounding in reality,

00:41:23.630 --> 00:41:26.980
it's still often the
case that that's true,

00:41:26.980 --> 00:41:29.130
I think over the last
10 or 15 years actually,

00:41:29.130 --> 00:41:32.660
I've noticed a lot more
willingness of people to talk

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:35.430
and the engagement of churches
and books being written

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:38.580
and being podcast and being published,

00:41:38.580 --> 00:41:41.020
where Christians are talking
about their own experience

00:41:41.020 --> 00:41:44.810
of mental illness, where
Christians are calling the church

00:41:44.810 --> 00:41:48.200
to be more active and engaged
and so that's all really good

00:41:48.200 --> 00:41:49.430
and that's happening

00:41:49.430 --> 00:41:51.430
in a variety of different church contexts,

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:53.780
that's happened in a
variety of different ways,

00:41:53.780 --> 00:41:56.750
including in Black church settings,

00:41:56.750 --> 00:41:59.900
so Monica Coleman's book,
"Bipolar Faith" has been something

00:41:59.900 --> 00:42:03.030
that a lot of my students
have found really helpful

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:07.167
in de-stigmatizing mental
illness in the Black community

00:42:07.167 --> 00:42:10.180
and I think that all of
that is really important.

00:42:10.180 --> 00:42:13.700
I think again, kinda going
back to Dr. Hankerson's work,

00:42:13.700 --> 00:42:16.360
I think one of the challenge
is when the church does begin

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:19.820
to talk about mental health
problems, how not to make it

00:42:19.820 --> 00:42:22.330
just a matter of individual
people having problems

00:42:22.330 --> 00:42:26.340
but how do we both recognize
that mental illness

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:28.650
and mental health challenges
are deeply individual

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:31.150
and we can provide help to
individuals and to families

00:42:31.150 --> 00:42:32.750
and also to locate them

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:36.380
in a broader social, cultural
context, like what's happening

00:42:36.380 --> 00:42:39.240
in our, with our policies
and with our programs

00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:41.150
and with the way our
communities are structured

00:42:41.150 --> 00:42:43.540
and things like housing that
might give rise to that.

00:42:43.540 --> 00:42:46.210
And I think that's where
some of the conversations

00:42:46.210 --> 00:42:50.181
around church and mental
health need to expand some

00:42:50.181 --> 00:42:52.830
to have a kind of broader
social perspective

00:42:52.830 --> 00:42:55.730
so it's not just a matter of
individual treatment-seeking

00:42:55.730 --> 00:42:58.130
although that's really important.

00:42:58.130 --> 00:42:59.287
- Yeah, I think that's really well said

00:42:59.287 --> 00:43:01.460
and it's helpful to hear
some of those parallels

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:04.350
and just to know what you're
equipping your students with

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:06.880
as well, and I think all
these pieces of conversation

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:08.880
are so, so powerful.

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:11.400
I'm curious for those who are listening,

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:12.780
from two sides who may be listening,

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:14.260
who may be a member of a faith community

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:16.330
or a member of a church and are listening

00:43:16.330 --> 00:43:17.790
and saying that all of
these things sound great

00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:19.340
and they sound like
they're great initiatives

00:43:19.340 --> 00:43:20.760
and it's good to be
able to have the vision

00:43:20.760 --> 00:43:22.360
and the plugin of resources

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:26.120
but those who feel isolated
and don't know where to start.

00:43:26.120 --> 00:43:28.970
So people who may have
tried to approach questions

00:43:28.970 --> 00:43:30.050
of mental health within the church

00:43:30.050 --> 00:43:31.610
and feel like they're getting pushed back

00:43:31.610 --> 00:43:32.850
and they don't know where to go to,

00:43:32.850 --> 00:43:36.000
do any of you have advice
for someone in that situation

00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:37.940
about how to move forward

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:40.120
and then at the same
time, what if that person

00:43:40.120 --> 00:43:41.070
is actually a leader?

00:43:41.070 --> 00:43:43.120
So someone who's in a leadership position

00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:46.210
but feels like the leadership
is not in line with them,

00:43:46.210 --> 00:43:47.330
where do those individuals

00:43:47.330 --> 00:43:49.970
and how do they really plug in

00:43:49.970 --> 00:43:52.033
to really get to a better place?

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:59.760
- I think, here's the reality,
there are a lot of churches

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:01.640
and we've all alluded to this earlier,

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:06.640
there are a lot of churches
where we have over-spiritualized

00:44:08.713 --> 00:44:11.740
what we know to be mental health issues

00:44:11.740 --> 00:44:16.430
and because of that, and because
many churches still operate

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:19.350
within certain theological traditions

00:44:19.350 --> 00:44:22.380
and certain theological frameworks,

00:44:22.380 --> 00:44:26.220
that tragically don't make
room for the possibility

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:29.940
that there is something here
at work beyond the demonic.

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:34.940
It is amazing how today in
2021, that with all the data,

00:44:35.740 --> 00:44:38.690
with all the research, with
all the work that is being done

00:44:38.690 --> 00:44:42.970
around mental health issues,
that there are still people

00:44:42.970 --> 00:44:46.440
who approach this with a
primitive frame of mind

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:51.440
and who do not acknowledge
that there are issues at stake

00:44:52.900 --> 00:44:57.900
that are not, that are
beyond the narrative

00:44:58.513 --> 00:45:01.560
of demon possession or
we need to pray this away

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:05.850
or we need to pray, fast and
tarry and all these things.

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:09.750
So I think for people who
are in those kinds of spaces,

00:45:09.750 --> 00:45:13.670
one, I don't think that they
ought to discount the reality

00:45:13.670 --> 00:45:15.890
that they live or know

00:45:15.890 --> 00:45:19.440
because what happens is that
when you enter certain spaces

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:22.030
that do not acknowledge these realities,

00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:25.860
there is a sense of dismissiveness
that can be destructive

00:45:27.020 --> 00:45:29.210
and even disastrous for people,

00:45:29.210 --> 00:45:31.970
not just who wanna talk
about it or not just people

00:45:31.970 --> 00:45:34.150
who are living in these spaces

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:37.350
because what churches
have to also acknowledge

00:45:37.350 --> 00:45:42.350
is that we have participated
in those who have been silenced

00:45:43.600 --> 00:45:46.510
and remain silent in their suffering

00:45:46.510 --> 00:45:51.280
because of sometimes the lack of empathy

00:45:51.280 --> 00:45:55.130
and even the punitive
language that emerges

00:45:55.130 --> 00:45:57.830
from some of our faith institutions.

00:45:57.830 --> 00:46:02.260
So what I would tell someone
is, do not let the behavior

00:46:02.260 --> 00:46:05.400
of a particular institution
become definitive

00:46:05.400 --> 00:46:08.590
for the possibilities of what can happen

00:46:08.590 --> 00:46:11.450
and like, as Dr. Kinghorn
said, you have to find a space

00:46:11.450 --> 00:46:16.340
and a place that values
that, that affirms this

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:18.690
that not only that affirms the reality

00:46:18.690 --> 00:46:19.670
of mental health issues

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:23.050
but also is doing what Dr.
Green alluded to earlier,

00:46:23.050 --> 00:46:25.890
you can pray and get therapy,

00:46:25.890 --> 00:46:29.780
you can be a deep believer
in God and seek treatment

00:46:29.780 --> 00:46:31.850
and I'm a witness to that.

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:34.790
And I also think that
one of the other things

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:35.950
that have to happen

00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:40.950
is, we've, I've gone, I've
been on panels, I've had talks

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:44.180
but I think there needs
to be gatherings as well

00:46:44.180 --> 00:46:45.840
and we're doing something like that,

00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:47.370
Dr. Green is helping in
doing something like this

00:46:47.370 --> 00:46:48.820
in the fall

00:46:48.820 --> 00:46:52.993
but gathering of clergy, being
able to pull clergy together,

00:46:54.280 --> 00:46:58.350
whether it is in even in seminary

00:46:58.350 --> 00:47:01.490
and I don't, I'm a proud
graduate of Duke Divinity School

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:05.410
but I don't know if
Warren Kinghorn was there

00:47:05.410 --> 00:47:06.900
when we had this conversation,

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:09.280
you have many divinity schools

00:47:09.280 --> 00:47:11.980
that no longer make a requirement,

00:47:11.980 --> 00:47:14.160
pastoral care and counseling to graduate

00:47:14.160 --> 00:47:16.320
with a degree in divinity,

00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:20.720
which is amazing because
now we have persons

00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:22.480
who are leading institutions

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:26.650
who may not have had even
a modicum of understanding

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:29.950
of issues around mental
health and mental wellness.

00:47:29.950 --> 00:47:33.600
So, I mean, those are some ways
but getting clergy together

00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:37.430
to begin to hear narratives
because what you will discover,

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:40.270
clergy too suffer in silence

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:45.170
because of the expectation
that people have of clergy

00:47:45.170 --> 00:47:48.180
to be the problem-solvers, the healers

00:47:48.180 --> 00:47:50.210
but as Dr. Green alluded
to, there comes a time

00:47:50.210 --> 00:47:52.350
when the healers need healing

00:47:52.350 --> 00:47:54.700
to deal with their own psychic fractures

00:47:54.700 --> 00:47:56.770
and I agree to it anecdotally,

00:47:56.770 --> 00:48:01.770
what really triggered me in
a major way to really commit

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:06.130
to this initiative of the HOPE
Center was a friend of mine,

00:48:06.130 --> 00:48:08.790
a good friend of mine that
I went to college with

00:48:08.790 --> 00:48:11.477
had another friend that
I knew marginally though

00:48:11.477 --> 00:48:13.380
but I knew of him.

00:48:13.380 --> 00:48:17.260
And on a Sunday morning, he
had two services at his church,

00:48:17.260 --> 00:48:20.230
an eight o'clock and an 11 o'clock service

00:48:20.230 --> 00:48:23.550
and on this particular
Sunday, his family, his wife

00:48:23.550 --> 00:48:25.800
and children, two children
were at home getting ready

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:27.480
and they missed the first service

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:29.530
and were coming to the 11 o'clock service.

00:48:29.530 --> 00:48:33.450
So he goes to church, he
preached the eight o'clock sermon

00:48:33.450 --> 00:48:34.750
for the service.

00:48:34.750 --> 00:48:37.390
He went home between services,

00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:38.660
they didn't live far from the church

00:48:38.660 --> 00:48:41.110
and his wife and kids were
getting ready, were ready

00:48:41.110 --> 00:48:42.940
and he said, "You all go ahead,
I'll meet you at the church

00:48:42.940 --> 00:48:44.720
I have to do a couple of things".

00:48:44.720 --> 00:48:47.790
And so the wife and the
children left to go to church,

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:50.180
he's getting ready to
preach the 11 o'clock sermon

00:48:50.180 --> 00:48:54.410
and left the house, went in
his car, went in the driveway,

00:48:54.410 --> 00:48:57.310
took out a gun and shot
himself in the head.

00:48:57.310 --> 00:49:00.350
So after preaching the
eight o'clock service,

00:49:00.350 --> 00:49:01.603
he committed suicide.

00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:06.853
And what was amazing is, is
that talking to my friend,

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:11.600
that at the funeral, no
one mentioned suicide,

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:12.750
no one mentioned

00:49:12.750 --> 00:49:14.470
that there was some kind
of depression going on

00:49:14.470 --> 00:49:16.480
'cause he had been battling
depression for some time

00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:18.800
and hadn't really, only
shared it with a few friends.

00:49:18.800 --> 00:49:22.310
There was as if he just
died of natural causes

00:49:22.310 --> 00:49:25.970
and no conversation during,
or no con after or before

00:49:25.970 --> 00:49:28.140
about what this man had
been suffering with.

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:31.480
That is because the
family was still hesitant,

00:49:31.480 --> 00:49:35.280
I imagine of the stigma
connected that a pastor

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:39.150
'cause the idea is if you trust
God, if you believe in God,

00:49:39.150 --> 00:49:41.070
why, and you lean into God in prayer,

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:42.840
why would you need to commit suicide?

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:45.430
And so the people surrounding him

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:49.530
still bought into that narrative
and were almost ashamed

00:49:49.530 --> 00:49:53.073
to speak about the fact that
this pastor committed suicide.

00:49:54.320 --> 00:49:56.603
If you look at some of the
numbers, I know Dr. Kinghorn,

00:49:56.603 --> 00:49:59.070
I mean, some years ago, I
think Duke had done a study

00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:00.970
about the number of clergy

00:50:00.970 --> 00:50:02.770
who were actually committing suicide

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:06.190
and the clergy, amount
of clergy studies show

00:50:06.190 --> 00:50:07.980
about who, are suffering depression.

00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:10.470
And I mean, it is amazing

00:50:10.470 --> 00:50:13.300
but again, if there's any group
of people who are suffering

00:50:13.300 --> 00:50:17.060
in silence, it is pastors who
have these great expectations

00:50:17.060 --> 00:50:19.210
to keep them, pawn them from congregations

00:50:19.210 --> 00:50:23.010
that they're almost invincible
and nothing can penetrate

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:26.277
the strength that they have
been endowed with by God

00:50:26.277 --> 00:50:28.563
and so they suffer in silence.

00:50:30.700 --> 00:50:33.517
- Yeah, that's so
important, that so important

00:50:33.517 --> 00:50:35.480
and I appreciate you bringing that out

00:50:35.480 --> 00:50:37.990
and just, I mean, so
much that needs to be...

00:50:37.990 --> 00:50:41.800
is that something that you all
have tried to tackle head-on

00:50:41.800 --> 00:50:43.370
in the work in the HOPE Center?

00:50:43.370 --> 00:50:45.110
I mean, what do you feel like is different

00:50:45.110 --> 00:50:46.610
about what you're doing there?

00:50:48.646 --> 00:50:51.417
- I would say directly
in terms of clergy, no

00:50:52.760 --> 00:50:56.773
but I think what we've been
doing in the HOPE Center,

00:50:57.630 --> 00:50:59.037
I like what Dr. Green

00:50:59.037 --> 00:51:01.200
and I wonder if she'll talk more about it

00:51:02.154 --> 00:51:03.700
but there's clinical work

00:51:03.700 --> 00:51:08.700
but I think where a lot of
the traction has taken place

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:11.420
with regard to change in the narrative,

00:51:11.420 --> 00:51:14.830
is a lot of the public-facing
work she talked about.

00:51:14.830 --> 00:51:17.663
I think that is to me,

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:21.130
there's no, that's where
a lot of the weight

00:51:21.130 --> 00:51:24.680
of the potential we have
for impact really goes

00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:26.570
and I would love to
her expand on that more

00:51:26.570 --> 00:51:27.770
because of what that means.

00:51:27.770 --> 00:51:31.630
When we have these programs,
these wellness check-ins

00:51:31.630 --> 00:51:33.810
and the like, and I let
her talk more about that,

00:51:33.810 --> 00:51:37.260
I think it goes a long way in
terms of the public realizing

00:51:37.260 --> 00:51:40.477
that this institution is
about more than just saying,

00:51:40.477 --> 00:51:42.540
"Hey, if you have something
going on mentally,

00:51:42.540 --> 00:51:43.373
come over to us".

00:51:43.373 --> 00:51:46.823
No it's about life and
wellness and wellbeing.

00:51:48.150 --> 00:51:49.220
- Yeah, so on that note, Dr. Green,

00:51:49.220 --> 00:51:52.543
how are people responding to
the public-facing component?

00:51:54.030 --> 00:51:57.070
- Our response I think,
has been really great,

00:51:57.070 --> 00:51:59.993
I would say at the
beginning of the pandemic,

00:52:00.870 --> 00:52:05.040
the HOPE Center engaged
into virtual opportunity

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:06.350
that both happened on Facebook.

00:52:06.350 --> 00:52:08.960
So we started doing mindfulness moments,

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:12.590
so 30 minutes of
mindfulness-based practices,

00:52:12.590 --> 00:52:17.590
so anything from breath
work, to yoga, to meditation,

00:52:19.020 --> 00:52:21.420
so that happens every Tuesday

00:52:21.420 --> 00:52:25.090
and then every Thursday, we
have our wellness check-ins

00:52:25.090 --> 00:52:26.950
again, those are virtually as well

00:52:26.950 --> 00:52:28.457
and people are able to just pop on

00:52:28.457 --> 00:52:32.570
and just sort of get a
word, an encouraging word

00:52:32.570 --> 00:52:35.051
for 15 to 20 minutes from someone

00:52:35.051 --> 00:52:36.500
and we have an array of
people that participate,

00:52:36.500 --> 00:52:38.280
so they're either pastors,

00:52:38.280 --> 00:52:41.117
they might be a motivational
speaker, they are our interns,

00:52:41.117 --> 00:52:46.117
our clinical interns here, I've
done a couple when I have to

00:52:47.570 --> 00:52:48.480
but we invite folks

00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:50.600
who have just sort of
like upbeat personality

00:52:50.600 --> 00:52:53.380
and then also clinicians to join us

00:52:53.380 --> 00:52:55.440
to do those virtual moments twice a week

00:52:55.440 --> 00:52:56.940
and those have been great.

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:57.940
We've also partnered

00:52:57.940 --> 00:52:59.940
with an organization called MYX Fitness,

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:02.090
so they've been joining us

00:53:02.090 --> 00:53:07.090
because they are a group of
wellness and fitness instructors

00:53:07.090 --> 00:53:08.770
who come on once a month

00:53:08.770 --> 00:53:11.810
and participate in our
virtual mindfulness moments

00:53:11.810 --> 00:53:13.749
and share something with us.

00:53:13.749 --> 00:53:15.530
So whether it's fitness,
whether it's stretching,

00:53:15.530 --> 00:53:20.530
whether it's tapping
meditation for anxiety,

00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:23.310
so it's been great,

00:53:23.310 --> 00:53:25.740
we've gotten a lot of
positive feedback on that

00:53:25.740 --> 00:53:30.560
and also to those
sessions live on Facebook

00:53:30.560 --> 00:53:33.240
and so people can share them
or where they can watch them

00:53:33.240 --> 00:53:34.900
as often as they'd like.

00:53:34.900 --> 00:53:36.710
So those have been fantastic for us

00:53:36.710 --> 00:53:37.543
and so

00:53:38.410 --> 00:53:41.060
we're trying to think
about how do we continue

00:53:41.060 --> 00:53:44.317
to offer these things as we
move sort of out of the pandemic

00:53:44.317 --> 00:53:46.847
and the world opens up a little bit more,

00:53:46.847 --> 00:53:49.650
what does that look like now
that people have to go in?

00:53:49.650 --> 00:53:50.860
The other things that we've been doing

00:53:50.860 --> 00:53:53.800
that are public-facing
are just doing groups,

00:53:53.800 --> 00:53:56.800
so we've had some fantastic
response to our group

00:53:56.800 --> 00:53:59.670
so we did a group for new
and expectant parents,

00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:01.511
an (murmurs) group that sort of focused

00:54:01.511 --> 00:54:06.511
on an array of different issues
that are important to moms,

00:54:06.700 --> 00:54:10.203
whether it's getting ready for
baby, postpartum depression,

00:54:11.270 --> 00:54:13.550
understanding the ways
in which you can get care

00:54:13.550 --> 00:54:16.270
including adding a
doula, which is important

00:54:16.270 --> 00:54:19.920
because for particularly for
women of color, Black women

00:54:20.860 --> 00:54:23.390
there's an issue around
maternal mortality rates

00:54:23.390 --> 00:54:25.370
and we wanna figure out how
to support them around that

00:54:25.370 --> 00:54:28.250
and as well as their
maternal mental health.

00:54:28.250 --> 00:54:32.280
We provided a CBT-based
skills group as well

00:54:32.280 --> 00:54:34.970
that actually had been the
most popular group to date

00:54:34.970 --> 00:54:37.333
and again, all of our services are free,

00:54:38.210 --> 00:54:41.390
from May, we're gonna be
doing wellness Wednesdays,

00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:42.960
so that's gonna be a four-week series

00:54:42.960 --> 00:54:46.000
and then in June, we have
something just for the guys

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:48.650
that's gonna be focused
on unmasking masculinity

00:54:48.650 --> 00:54:51.783
and that's gonna be led
by our male therapist here

00:54:51.783 --> 00:54:54.520
and we perhaps need get another topic.

00:54:54.520 --> 00:54:56.980
So, and then we just finished
our HOPE Center weekend

00:54:56.980 --> 00:54:58.730
where Pastor Mike

00:54:58.730 --> 00:55:03.040
and our in-house male
clinician were able to focus

00:55:03.040 --> 00:55:05.160
on a men's talk.

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:07.700
So we're figuring out ways
in which he can again,

00:55:07.700 --> 00:55:11.790
bump up the public-facing opportunities

00:55:11.790 --> 00:55:14.220
because we know that people
may not necessarily be ready

00:55:14.220 --> 00:55:15.940
to start individual therapy

00:55:15.940 --> 00:55:17.360
but in all of those opportunities,

00:55:17.360 --> 00:55:20.980
we certainly make the pitch
for it, we provide resources,

00:55:20.980 --> 00:55:25.570
so we have a number where
folks can text to get services,

00:55:25.570 --> 00:55:28.510
we always provide our national
suicide hotline number

00:55:28.510 --> 00:55:31.860
where folks can call and
then at any given point,

00:55:31.860 --> 00:55:34.820
we provide resources to all
of the groups in the program

00:55:34.820 --> 00:55:35.653
that we provide.

00:55:35.653 --> 00:55:38.290
So we try to continue to keep
the conversation that way

00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:41.230
and remind folks that services are here,

00:55:41.230 --> 00:55:44.920
free here at the HOPE Center
and that they can call

00:55:44.920 --> 00:55:48.390
and, or go online and sign
up to get an appointment

00:55:48.390 --> 00:55:50.806
and it's free and confidential.

00:55:50.806 --> 00:55:53.300
And so that is I think
the important messages

00:55:53.300 --> 00:55:55.560
that we try to share consistently

00:55:55.560 --> 00:55:58.580
so that folks know that
there's a resource available.

00:55:58.580 --> 00:56:00.210
- Yeah, and again, that's
so across the board

00:56:00.210 --> 00:56:01.710
in terms of topics and all those pieces,

00:56:01.710 --> 00:56:03.150
I think that's wonderful.

00:56:03.150 --> 00:56:04.690
How are people walking into that space?

00:56:04.690 --> 00:56:06.730
I mean, is there, are people walking in

00:56:06.730 --> 00:56:09.420
in kind of in a, they are creeping in

00:56:09.420 --> 00:56:12.150
and don't wanna be seen, are
people open and enthusiastic,

00:56:12.150 --> 00:56:13.060
is it a mixture of both?

00:56:13.060 --> 00:56:14.704
Because I mean, when
you talked about that,

00:56:14.704 --> 00:56:15.990
some of the conversations that you have,

00:56:15.990 --> 00:56:17.476
trying to break that stigma

00:56:17.476 --> 00:56:19.474
and there's so many rich
resources you're providing,

00:56:19.474 --> 00:56:21.070
I'm just curious how people,
how they are entering in

00:56:21.070 --> 00:56:22.020
in the first place.

00:56:23.130 --> 00:56:24.970
- So everyone's virtually entering in.

00:56:24.970 --> 00:56:25.803
- True.

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:29.570
- Our doors aren't open right now

00:56:29.570 --> 00:56:33.030
although there's a number
of the staff who are here

00:56:33.030 --> 00:56:35.880
during the week, so our
interns, our executive assistant

00:56:35.880 --> 00:56:37.940
and myself are here during the week

00:56:37.940 --> 00:56:39.750
but all of our services are virtual

00:56:39.750 --> 00:56:41.170
and people can actually call,

00:56:41.170 --> 00:56:42.700
so they can call and get a live person

00:56:42.700 --> 00:56:44.240
and have to actually speak to someone

00:56:44.240 --> 00:56:46.920
or they can go on and
sign up confidentially

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:51.410
and then I go ahead and
assign someone to a therapist,

00:56:51.410 --> 00:56:54.102
assign a potential
innovator to a therapist

00:56:54.102 --> 00:56:56.060
and then they go ahead and
get a phone call, an email,

00:56:56.060 --> 00:56:58.360
so we do about three to
four outreach attempts

00:56:59.900 --> 00:57:03.460
in an effort to get them
engaged into therapy

00:57:03.460 --> 00:57:05.290
and then if not, they go
back onto the waiting list

00:57:05.290 --> 00:57:07.473
as we continue to have a waiting list.

00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:12.380
And even with myself, we
also have a psychologist,

00:57:12.380 --> 00:57:14.270
we work with Dr. Hankerson,
who's a psychiatrist

00:57:14.270 --> 00:57:16.650
and then we've had nine interns,

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:19.310
so even with nine interns
and the clinical staff,

00:57:19.310 --> 00:57:21.776
we still had a significant waiting list.

00:57:21.776 --> 00:57:23.593
So that's why we operate the groups too

00:57:23.593 --> 00:57:26.670
so people have an
opportunity to get engaged.

00:57:26.670 --> 00:57:28.207
- Well, that's great and in a lot of ways,

00:57:28.207 --> 00:57:30.400
it seems like, I mean, in
some ways the virtual piece

00:57:30.400 --> 00:57:34.550
may have helped to give
people that space to not...

00:57:34.550 --> 00:57:36.450
I mean, not that the stigma
doesn't have to be dealt with

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:38.427
at some point but just to
really to be able to enter in

00:57:38.427 --> 00:57:40.980
and I would imagine that
having the experience

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:43.160
may have also helped as
people are getting exposed

00:57:43.160 --> 00:57:45.520
to all these resources, may
even help break down that stigma

00:57:45.520 --> 00:57:48.550
and make other
opportunities going forward.

00:57:48.550 --> 00:57:50.320
So, and as you hinted,

00:57:50.320 --> 00:57:53.310
I guess that will be a challenge
coming back into the world

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:57.130
in a sense to see how that
dynamic will go forward.

00:57:57.130 --> 00:57:59.250
- Yeah, I certainly think

00:57:59.250 --> 00:58:01.933
that virtual programming is here to stay,

00:58:02.892 --> 00:58:05.019
I don't think that we'll ever go back

00:58:05.019 --> 00:58:06.070
to non-virtual services,

00:58:06.070 --> 00:58:07.980
whether it's for medical appointments

00:58:07.980 --> 00:58:09.620
or for mental health appointments,

00:58:09.620 --> 00:58:12.680
I think we've all had some,
a major learning curve

00:58:12.680 --> 00:58:15.880
during the pandemic around how
do we provide these services,

00:58:15.880 --> 00:58:18.173
good quality services in a virtual space.

00:58:19.307 --> 00:58:21.560
And so I would say prior to the pandemic,

00:58:21.560 --> 00:58:24.110
I was probably a therapist
that was on the fence

00:58:24.110 --> 00:58:27.740
about being in the room with someone

00:58:27.740 --> 00:58:31.960
because of all the things that
you receive from one another

00:58:31.960 --> 00:58:35.100
as you're working towards
your wellness journey.

00:58:35.100 --> 00:58:37.740
But certainly been able to
do that in a virtual space

00:58:37.740 --> 00:58:39.725
and I'm grateful for that opportunity,

00:58:39.725 --> 00:58:41.053
so we've all been Zooming.

00:58:42.540 --> 00:58:43.900
- Yeah, that makes sense in a lot of ways

00:58:43.900 --> 00:58:46.760
and even thinking back
to those individuals

00:58:46.760 --> 00:58:48.830
who may feel like they
don't have that support

00:58:48.830 --> 00:58:49.965
in their own...

00:58:49.965 --> 00:58:50.890
I mean, I don't know if
you've seen that at all

00:58:50.890 --> 00:58:53.170
from people from other faith communities,

00:58:53.170 --> 00:58:55.180
again, getting back to that
Pastor Michael mentioned,

00:58:55.180 --> 00:58:57.380
who feel like they can step into this

00:58:57.380 --> 00:58:58.323
and maybe a different
faith-based community

00:58:58.323 --> 00:59:00.829
that has moved the conversation forward

00:59:00.829 --> 00:59:02.680
and that has some of
those pieces available,

00:59:02.680 --> 00:59:05.550
so I think those things are
options for people as well

00:59:05.550 --> 00:59:07.540
so that can also provide
a lot of opportunity

00:59:07.540 --> 00:59:09.870
that wasn't there in
the past in some ways.

00:59:09.870 --> 00:59:11.670
- Yeah, absolutely and I've been able

00:59:11.670 --> 00:59:16.670
to do some presentations
with some community of faith,

00:59:17.060 --> 00:59:19.300
whether it's through the mayor's office

00:59:19.300 --> 00:59:23.560
or the consortium through
one of the hospitals

00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:26.810
and so folks I think are a
little bit open to having me come

00:59:26.810 --> 00:59:28.710
and present and talk about the HOPE Center

00:59:28.710 --> 00:59:30.670
and the services that we provide here

00:59:30.670 --> 00:59:33.710
and while our, Pastor Mike
shared that at the HOPE Center

00:59:33.710 --> 00:59:35.590
was originally created to provide services

00:59:35.590 --> 00:59:39.160
to the congregation, we
don't turn anyone away.

00:59:39.160 --> 00:59:42.890
So we have people who
are receiving services

00:59:42.890 --> 00:59:45.910
who aren't members of our FCBC community

00:59:45.910 --> 00:59:49.990
but they may be in Paris
or Germany or Ireland

00:59:49.990 --> 00:59:53.350
and so we're, I'm
excited that we're global

00:59:53.350 --> 00:59:57.090
and that folks can touch
base and get treatment

00:59:57.090 --> 00:59:58.770
no matter where they are.

00:59:58.770 --> 00:59:59.990
- Yeah, that sounds so powerful

00:59:59.990 --> 01:00:01.853
and expansion in some ways as well.

01:00:03.148 --> 01:00:05.607
And so, I mean, this is so,
this is such a rich conversation

01:00:05.607 --> 01:00:09.170
and so many pieces to pull out
and again, I'm just grateful

01:00:09.170 --> 01:00:10.630
for some of the practical contents

01:00:10.630 --> 01:00:11.779
that you've talked about as well

01:00:11.779 --> 01:00:12.612
and so I think for our listeners

01:00:12.612 --> 01:00:14.390
a lot of those will go a long way,

01:00:14.390 --> 01:00:15.980
specifically for those who feel isolated

01:00:15.980 --> 01:00:17.780
in their specific situations.

01:00:17.780 --> 01:00:20.750
But as we continue, or start to wrap up,

01:00:20.750 --> 01:00:24.770
Dr. Kinghorn I was gonna ask
you, from your perspective

01:00:24.770 --> 01:00:27.390
as someone, again, on both
sides, both a psychiatrist

01:00:27.390 --> 01:00:30.040
and a theologian who's
working at Divinity School

01:00:30.040 --> 01:00:32.500
and as someone who's admired
the work of the HOPE Center,

01:00:32.500 --> 01:00:35.050
what do you, what is giving you hope

01:00:35.050 --> 01:00:37.213
in the work that you're doing
yourself and in the work

01:00:37.213 --> 01:00:40.090
that you're seeing Pastor
Mike and Dr. Green doing

01:00:40.090 --> 01:00:42.290
in terms of how we're
moving things forward?

01:00:43.208 --> 01:00:44.363
- It's a great question.

01:00:44.363 --> 01:00:48.800
I think I am feeling, I think
it's important to hold hope

01:00:48.800 --> 01:00:50.070
and challenge together

01:00:50.070 --> 01:00:52.600
that you can only really
understand what hope is

01:00:52.600 --> 01:00:55.300
when you are willing to see
the enormity of challenge.

01:00:56.300 --> 01:00:58.433
And I think that both,

01:00:59.418 --> 01:01:00.647
in terms of what we've
been describing today

01:01:00.647 --> 01:01:01.760
and in terms of Christian faith, I think

01:01:01.760 --> 01:01:04.590
that there is, we have a lot of resources.

01:01:04.590 --> 01:01:06.890
So on one hand, I think that
the challenges are immense

01:01:06.890 --> 01:01:09.240
and especially the challenges
in the pandemic are immense,

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:12.370
everything that's happening
in the world all around us

01:01:12.370 --> 01:01:13.919
I also though think

01:01:13.919 --> 01:01:17.220
that it's tempting in the
midst of big challenges

01:01:17.220 --> 01:01:19.460
to hit despair, to think
like this is just the way

01:01:19.460 --> 01:01:21.580
it's going to be, we
just have to hunker down,

01:01:21.580 --> 01:01:23.160
we just have to push through

01:01:23.160 --> 01:01:27.110
and I think our Christian faith teaches us

01:01:27.110 --> 01:01:31.020
that we are loved by God before all else

01:01:31.020 --> 01:01:33.080
and that humans are created with dignity

01:01:33.080 --> 01:01:35.880
and in image of God and that...

01:01:35.880 --> 01:01:39.460
and also that God is already
doing the work of healing

01:01:39.460 --> 01:01:41.070
before us and around us

01:01:41.070 --> 01:01:43.440
and that we don't generate this ourselves

01:01:43.440 --> 01:01:46.220
through our own like energy and time

01:01:46.220 --> 01:01:49.270
and, as if we're (murmurs)
but we're participating

01:01:49.270 --> 01:01:53.020
in God's ongoing healing
work in the world.

01:01:53.020 --> 01:01:55.780
And so as a psychiatrist
who's also a Christian,

01:01:55.780 --> 01:01:58.750
I wanna say, how do I just
kind of join in that lane

01:01:58.750 --> 01:02:00.100
to like join in the work

01:02:00.100 --> 01:02:03.470
that the Holy Spirit is
already doing in the world

01:02:03.470 --> 01:02:07.670
and that takes maybe some of
the felt pressure off of me

01:02:07.670 --> 01:02:09.610
but it also puts pressure on me

01:02:09.610 --> 01:02:11.747
to like continue to kind
of seek out that space

01:02:11.747 --> 01:02:13.290
and that area.

01:02:13.290 --> 01:02:17.520
And so I think, and I also
think that as a psychiatrist,

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:18.900
I can often get in the mode of fixing,

01:02:18.900 --> 01:02:20.530
like patients presenting
me with a problem,

01:02:20.530 --> 01:02:22.196
I got to fix them.

01:02:22.196 --> 01:02:24.420
And I think that our faith gives us

01:02:24.420 --> 01:02:27.690
a different kind of frame
that we are not machines

01:02:27.690 --> 01:02:30.813
that need to be fixed but we
are those who are on a journey,

01:02:31.820 --> 01:02:35.906
from God to God and that this
life is a kind of journey

01:02:35.906 --> 01:02:37.430
through life to God

01:02:37.430 --> 01:02:39.360
and we walk, we ourselves are on a journey

01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:43.540
and anyone who we try to help
or to minister to or with

01:02:43.540 --> 01:02:44.530
is also on a journey,

01:02:44.530 --> 01:02:48.407
so we walk as Wayfair is
alongside others on a journey.

01:02:48.407 --> 01:02:51.680
And so we can ask ourselves
like, what's needed right now

01:02:51.680 --> 01:02:53.970
for the journey and that
makes it very practical

01:02:53.970 --> 01:02:56.310
because what's needed
might be a medication

01:02:56.310 --> 01:02:59.110
or what's needed might
be a course of ECT or TMS

01:02:59.110 --> 01:03:01.250
or what's needed might
be a 12-session course

01:03:01.250 --> 01:03:03.840
of psychotherapy or what's
needed might be a men's group

01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:05.340
or what's needed might be a meal

01:03:05.340 --> 01:03:07.800
or to get out of an abusive relationship.

01:03:07.800 --> 01:03:11.080
And so I think that being a
Christian gives me permission

01:03:11.080 --> 01:03:13.190
to think broadly about
this kinds of things

01:03:13.190 --> 01:03:17.720
and to, and hope that this
work didn't start with me or us

01:03:17.720 --> 01:03:21.950
or any of us on this episode,
it doesn't end with us

01:03:21.950 --> 01:03:23.460
but it's going on and we get a chance

01:03:23.460 --> 01:03:24.917
to participate in that.

01:03:24.917 --> 01:03:27.100
And that gives me hope and
then just to see witnesses

01:03:27.100 --> 01:03:29.910
like Pastor Mike and Dr. Green and others

01:03:29.910 --> 01:03:31.870
in other places doing
really creative work,

01:03:31.870 --> 01:03:35.100
I look at that and I think,
man, this is so deeply needed

01:03:35.100 --> 01:03:39.150
and to be able just to see
that unfold and to celebrate it

01:03:39.150 --> 01:03:40.293
is something that I think

01:03:40.293 --> 01:03:42.193
is just really profoundly encouraging.

01:03:43.150 --> 01:03:44.420
- Wow, that's so well said

01:03:44.420 --> 01:03:46.760
and I just love that approach
too and the framework

01:03:46.760 --> 01:03:48.710
of really just looking
at things holistically

01:03:48.710 --> 01:03:51.860
and bringing all aspects
and all tools to the table,

01:03:51.860 --> 01:03:53.960
so I definitely appreciate
you sharing that

01:03:53.960 --> 01:03:55.260
and I know you're being humble

01:03:55.260 --> 01:03:56.850
and I know you're deeply
involved in that work

01:03:56.850 --> 01:03:58.070
in so many ways as well,

01:03:58.070 --> 01:04:00.587
so I deeply appreciate it for
the way that you're doing that

01:04:00.587 --> 01:04:03.390
and the way that you're
impacting the next generation

01:04:03.390 --> 01:04:06.520
of folks who will be doing
this both as clinicians,

01:04:06.520 --> 01:04:09.270
as psychiatrists, as pastors and teachers,

01:04:09.270 --> 01:04:10.880
so I also just want to acknowledge that

01:04:10.880 --> 01:04:13.810
and make sure that that's acknowledged

01:04:13.810 --> 01:04:16.310
and known to our audience as well.

01:04:16.310 --> 01:04:19.360
- Thank you, Dr. Nii and all
of us I think are in that role

01:04:19.360 --> 01:04:21.663
on this call, so I thank you.

01:04:22.600 --> 01:04:23.950
- Dr. Green, what about you?

01:04:23.950 --> 01:04:26.560
Even that juxtaposition goes
back to what you mentioned

01:04:26.560 --> 01:04:29.010
at the beginning about even
your own personal challenges

01:04:29.010 --> 01:04:32.580
in the midst of the hope
that you have as a Christian

01:04:32.580 --> 01:04:34.360
and the hope that you're
providing in your work,

01:04:34.360 --> 01:04:37.060
how do you continue to think
about that going forward?

01:04:38.373 --> 01:04:43.373
- So, Dr. Kinghorn said
it so perfectly too,

01:04:43.966 --> 01:04:46.033
I think about how do we do this work

01:04:47.299 --> 01:04:48.807
and how are we called to do this work?

01:04:48.807 --> 01:04:52.300
And we're always called to
do it in relationship, right?

01:04:52.300 --> 01:04:54.130
And so that's what really is at the heart

01:04:54.130 --> 01:04:57.660
of what we're trying to
do in therapy and groups

01:04:57.660 --> 01:05:00.520
on this healing journey, is
that I'm walking alongside you

01:05:00.520 --> 01:05:03.250
in this relationship
on this healing journey

01:05:03.250 --> 01:05:07.550
and how can I support you
on this and how do I learn

01:05:07.550 --> 01:05:10.820
because I tell folks all
the time when they come here

01:05:10.820 --> 01:05:13.620
and they, Dr. Kinghorn
mentioned, we wanna fix

01:05:13.620 --> 01:05:17.993
but also our clients or our
innovators expect us to fix it.

01:05:18.990 --> 01:05:22.420
And so I also say to people,
"Well, you're the expert

01:05:22.420 --> 01:05:24.230
on your own life.

01:05:24.230 --> 01:05:27.370
I'm here to listen, I'm here
to maybe pick out some things

01:05:27.370 --> 01:05:29.850
that maybe you didn't,
you weren't aware of

01:05:29.850 --> 01:05:32.460
or to challenge you in
new and different ways

01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:35.730
or expand your thinking ways
but you're the expert on that

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:38.170
and we're both learning
in this space together".

01:05:38.170 --> 01:05:42.720
And so I think that that's
an important lens to have

01:05:42.720 --> 01:05:45.230
in working with with people
who are on this journey

01:05:45.230 --> 01:05:48.660
to healing and then also
just continually thinking

01:05:48.660 --> 01:05:52.840
about how do I personally
intentionally cultivate spaces

01:05:52.840 --> 01:05:54.240
of joy?

01:05:54.240 --> 01:05:56.050
What does that look like for me on a daily

01:05:56.050 --> 01:05:57.660
and consistent basis?

01:05:57.660 --> 01:06:00.310
And I don't, sometimes I
don't do it every single day

01:06:01.350 --> 01:06:04.180
but reminding myself to
be intentional about it,

01:06:04.180 --> 01:06:06.960
reminding the people around
me to be intentional about it

01:06:06.960 --> 01:06:08.753
and holding myself accountable.

01:06:10.760 --> 01:06:13.930
- Yeah, that's so well said,
I love that relational piece

01:06:13.930 --> 01:06:16.250
and holding ourselves accountable to joy.

01:06:16.250 --> 01:06:18.673
I mean, I think that goes such a long way.

01:06:19.830 --> 01:06:21.490
Pastor Mike, to wrap up,

01:06:21.490 --> 01:06:23.750
there's so much that's been said here,

01:06:23.750 --> 01:06:25.130
I guess not even just hope for yourself

01:06:25.130 --> 01:06:27.400
but you should be able to
encourage our listeners too

01:06:27.400 --> 01:06:28.710
in ways to continue to find hope

01:06:28.710 --> 01:06:30.640
'cause I'm sure there are
those who are listening,

01:06:30.640 --> 01:06:33.360
who are struggling and who
are trying to find a way out.

01:06:33.360 --> 01:06:36.540
Any word you want to
speak to them directly?

01:06:36.540 --> 01:06:41.540
- Yeah, there's a, many years
ago, a line I heard or I read

01:06:41.770 --> 01:06:46.770
by late Howard Thurman, who has served

01:06:47.070 --> 01:06:50.370
as one of my own spiritual
and theological mentors

01:06:50.370 --> 01:06:53.840
along my journey as a
believer and as a pastor

01:06:53.840 --> 01:06:55.320
and he was frequently saying

01:06:55.320 --> 01:06:58.410
that the contradictions
of life are not final.

01:06:58.410 --> 01:07:01.220
And I think in these past 18 months,

01:07:01.220 --> 01:07:06.000
we've seen many harsh
realities that have sought

01:07:06.000 --> 01:07:10.560
to, in some places, assault
life or undermine life

01:07:10.560 --> 01:07:15.560
and have given birth to many realities

01:07:17.260 --> 01:07:22.260
that many of us did not know
were even possibilities for us,

01:07:22.360 --> 01:07:27.360
meaning depressive moments,
moments filled with anxiety

01:07:28.130 --> 01:07:31.370
that this pandemic
brought to the forefront

01:07:31.370 --> 01:07:34.820
but I think it's always a
good reminder to tell people

01:07:34.820 --> 01:07:37.790
for me personally, that those
contradictions are not final,

01:07:37.790 --> 01:07:40.110
they do not hold the final word,

01:07:40.110 --> 01:07:42.720
they're not definitive on our lives,

01:07:42.720 --> 01:07:46.320
that we have not only power through God

01:07:46.320 --> 01:07:49.680
but transcendent power to overcome

01:07:49.680 --> 01:07:53.600
and see our way through
these kinds of seasons.

01:07:53.600 --> 01:07:57.090
One of our pastors, preached
a sermon last Sunday

01:07:57.090 --> 01:08:00.747
and she focused on Psalm 23

01:08:00.747 --> 01:08:03.670
and the particular verse
when the Psalmist says,

01:08:03.670 --> 01:08:06.310
David in particular, "Yea,
though I walk through the valley

01:08:06.310 --> 01:08:09.310
of the shadow of death,
I will fear no evil:

01:08:09.310 --> 01:08:10.727
for thou art with me",

01:08:11.611 --> 01:08:14.360
that confidence that the writer exhibits,

01:08:14.360 --> 01:08:19.360
that audacity is shaped
by an experience with God,

01:08:20.280 --> 01:08:23.470
for thou art with me and I
think at the end of the day,

01:08:23.470 --> 01:08:25.930
for believers who find themselves

01:08:25.930 --> 01:08:28.380
dealing with mental health challenges,

01:08:28.380 --> 01:08:31.680
we cannot forget the presence of God

01:08:31.680 --> 01:08:35.630
because it is through God I
think, that resources emerge,

01:08:35.630 --> 01:08:39.820
it is through God that
opportunities come to the forefront

01:08:39.820 --> 01:08:44.290
and it is also through God that
new and innovative pathways

01:08:44.290 --> 01:08:47.490
towards healing and
wholeness become visible.

01:08:47.490 --> 01:08:50.520
So those contradictions are not final,

01:08:50.520 --> 01:08:52.820
the best is yet to come

01:08:52.820 --> 01:08:55.373
and it does not appear what we shall be

01:08:55.373 --> 01:08:59.700
when we trust that we're being
led and guided and directed

01:08:59.700 --> 01:09:03.980
by a God who at the end of
the day, wants to see us

01:09:03.980 --> 01:09:05.680
be the best versions of ourselves.

01:09:08.200 --> 01:09:11.023
- Wow, so powerful, so well said.

01:09:12.588 --> 01:09:13.438
I mean, I'm in...

01:09:15.420 --> 01:09:17.820
well, I'm just in awe of just everything

01:09:17.820 --> 01:09:20.070
that we've gone through
and even what's happened

01:09:20.070 --> 01:09:21.800
in this last hour during our conversation

01:09:21.800 --> 01:09:25.310
and what's been happening over
the years at the HOPE Center

01:09:25.310 --> 01:09:27.890
and grateful, grateful for the trust

01:09:27.890 --> 01:09:29.800
that you all have put
in God and one another

01:09:29.800 --> 01:09:32.690
to be able to walk in
relationship like Dr. Green said

01:09:32.690 --> 01:09:33.523
in a lot of ways

01:09:33.523 --> 01:09:35.700
and walk in relationship
with those in your community,

01:09:35.700 --> 01:09:37.517
I think that's, it goes a long way

01:09:37.517 --> 01:09:41.180
and I'm sure that it's
important to the community

01:09:41.180 --> 01:09:43.500
and acknowledged, even in
times when it's challenging.

01:09:43.500 --> 01:09:47.107
So that gives me hope just
even hearing this story today

01:09:47.107 --> 01:09:48.900
and these stories that you all shared,

01:09:48.900 --> 01:09:51.887
so thank you so much,
Pastor Mike, Dr. Green

01:09:51.887 --> 01:09:55.980
and Dr. Kinghorn for jumping
on the "Addy Hour" podcast,

01:09:55.980 --> 01:09:57.480
I know this will be one of those

01:09:57.480 --> 01:09:59.460
that people are going to be
able to go back and listen to

01:09:59.460 --> 01:10:01.940
and I appreciate the way
you've spoken so poignantly

01:10:01.940 --> 01:10:05.360
to our listeners as well and
have been so real and honest

01:10:05.360 --> 01:10:06.373
at the same time.

01:10:07.630 --> 01:10:09.990
- Well, thank you for having
me and thank for having us,

01:10:09.990 --> 01:10:13.120
this has been great and I'm
grateful for the opportunity

01:10:13.120 --> 01:10:15.663
just to share and be part
of this conversation.

01:10:17.020 --> 01:10:18.363
- Thank you so much.

01:10:19.950 --> 01:10:22.472
- Well, great being with all of you

01:10:22.472 --> 01:10:24.030
and for all of our listeners, stay tuned

01:10:24.030 --> 01:10:26.060
for some upcoming episodes
of the "Addy Hour"

01:10:26.060 --> 01:10:28.120
and continue just like we
did today, to really dig deep

01:10:28.120 --> 01:10:29.400
into some of these topics

01:10:29.400 --> 01:10:31.390
and really have real
and honest conversations

01:10:31.390 --> 01:10:33.960
that I think can empower
us and encourage us all.

01:10:33.960 --> 01:10:35.063
So until next time.

01:10:35.917 --> 01:10:39.084
(bright upbeat music)

